title: Watch and ignore fields
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: reference
summary: mf_watch (inherited down the tree, false at the root) says what is tracked; mf_ignore patterns, from the nearest ancestor that has any, say what is left out.

Two fields on directory metarecords decide what is tracked. Both are inherited from the
nearest ancestor that defines them.

* ''`mf_watch`'' (`bool`) — whether this metarecord and what lies under it are eligible. Set on a directory, it governs the subtree; set on a file, it overrides for that file. The root always has it, `false` at creation: nothing is tracked until you whitelist something.
* ''`mf_ignore`'' (`string`, one row per pattern) — regular expressions tested against a candidate's path ''relative to its tracking scope'': the nearest ancestor on which `mf_watch` is set directly — the repository root in the common case, giving paths like `/music/.git/config`. The ''nearest ancestor with at least one row'' provides the whole set; sets from different ancestors are ''not merged''.

That last rule is the one trap: writing a single pattern on a directory that had none
replaces the whole inherited set for its subtree. The GUI offers to copy the inherited
set first ([[Setting ignore patterns]]); by hand, read the effective set, copy it onto
the directory, then edit.

The daemon writes `mf_watch = false` on a new root and no `mf_ignore` at all — it has
no built-in ignore policy. The familiar patterns come from the client, which applies the
`default` preset at `mf repo init` and at the GUI's repository creation
([[Ignore presets]]). Paths are repository-relative, so a repository can move without
invalidating its patterns.

The exact decision: [[Eligibility]].
